---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { COMPANY } from "../../../data/company";
---

<Layout
  title="Reverse Mortgages: Everything You Need to Know | Cliffco Mortgage Bankers"
  description="A complete guide to reverse mortgages (HECM) for homeowners 62 and older. Learn how they work, who qualifies, and when one makes sense."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Learn", href: "/learn/" },
    { name: "Reverse Mortgage Guide", href: "/learn/reverse-mortgage-guide/" },
  ]} />

  <section class="container-wide pt-12 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reverse Mortgage</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3" style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Reverse Mortgages: Everything You Need to Know</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-6">
      If you're 62 or older and own your home, a reverse mortgage lets you turn your equity into income
      without selling or making a monthly payment. Here's how they actually work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white pb-12">
    <div class="container-prose">

      <h2>What Is a Reverse Mortgage?</h2>
      <p>
        A reverse mortgage is a home loan designed for homeowners 62 and older that lets you access the
        equity you've built up in your home without selling it or making a monthly mortgage payment. Instead
        of you paying the lender, the lender pays you. That can come as a lump sum, a series of monthly
        payments, a line of credit you draw from as needed, or some combination of all three.
      </p>
      <p>
        The loan doesn't come due until you pass away, sell the home, or permanently move out. The vast
        majority of reverse mortgages in the U.S. are Home Equity Conversion Mortgages (HECMs), which
        are backed by the Federal Housing Administration (FHA) and carry certain consumer protections as
        a result.
      </p>

      <h2>How Does a Reverse Mortgage Work?</h2>
      <p>
        The process starts with an assessment of your home's value and your existing mortgage balance, if
        you have one. From there, you pick how you want to receive the funds. The key thing to understand
        is that the debt grows over time as interest accrues, because you're not making monthly payments
        to chip it down. Your home equity serves as the collateral.
      </p>
      <p>
        Older borrowers typically qualify for larger amounts because the lender calculates how long the
        loan will likely be outstanding. A 75-year-old homeowner will generally receive more than a
        62-year-old with the same home value, all else being equal.
      </p>

      <h2>Who Qualifies?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You must be at least 62 years old.</li>
        <li>The home must be your primary residence. Investment properties, vacation homes, and rentals do not qualify.</li>
        <li>You must own the home outright or have only a small remaining mortgage balance.</li>
        <li>You need to demonstrate the ability to keep up with property taxes, homeowners insurance, and basic maintenance.</li>
        <li>You must complete a HUD-approved counseling session before the loan can move forward.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That counseling requirement isn't just a formality. It's a real conversation with an independent
        advisor who helps you understand the full picture before you commit.
      </p>

      <h2>Good Candidates for a Reverse Mortgage</h2>
      <p>
        A reverse mortgage tends to make the most sense when you have significant equity built up, plan
        to stay in your home for the long term, and need to supplement a fixed income. Common situations
        where one genuinely helps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rising healthcare costs or in-home care expenses</li>
        <li>Home repairs or modifications for aging in place</li>
        <li>Bridging an income gap between retirement and Social Security</li>
        <li>Covering everyday living expenses without drawing down retirement accounts</li>
        <li>Preventing foreclosure when a traditional payment is no longer manageable</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When a Reverse Mortgage Is Not the Right Fit</h2>
      <p>
        It's just as important to know when a reverse mortgage doesn't make sense. If you have minimal
        equity, plan to move or downsize in the next few years, or are mainly motivated by wanting to
        leave the home's full value to your heirs, the costs may outweigh the benefits. The loan balance
        grows over time, which means less equity passes to your estate.
      </p>

      <h2>What About the Age Timing?</h2>
      <p>
        The minimum age is 62, but waiting longer typically unlocks a larger payout. If you're 62 and
        financially comfortable, it can still make sense to get started early, particularly if you want
        to delay tapping Social Security and let those benefits grow. A licensed Cliffco loan officer
        can walk through the scenarios with you.
      </p>

      <h2>What Are the Costs?</h2>
      <p>
        Reverse mortgages do have upfront costs: origination fees, standard closing costs, and FHA
        mortgage insurance premiums. Those can typically be rolled into the loan so you don't need cash
        at closing. What matters most is understanding that the loan balance grows over time as interest
        and fees accumulate. That's not a reason to avoid a reverse mortgage, but it's something to plan
        around.
      </p>

      <h2>What Happens When the Borrower Passes Away?</h2>
      <p>
        When the last borrower on the loan passes away or permanently leaves the home, the loan becomes
        due. In most cases, heirs settle it by selling the home. The family typically has around six
        months to do so. If the home's value is less than the loan balance at that point, FHA mortgage
        insurance covers the shortfall. No one in your family owes more than the home is worth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Non-borrowing spouses may be able to remain in the home under specific conditions set by HUD.
        This is a nuance worth discussing with your loan officer before you apply, especially if your
        spouse is under 62.
      </p>

      <h2>Can I Sell If I Have a Reverse Mortgage?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. The process looks like a normal home sale. You sell, the loan gets repaid from the proceeds,
        and any remaining equity goes to you (or your estate). If the sale price exceeds the loan balance,
        you keep the difference.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="accent-gradient">
    <div class="container-wide py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-white" style="font-size: var(--text-h2);">Ready to talk through your options?</h2>
      <p class="lead mt-4 text-white/85">A licensed Cliffco loan officer responds in one business day.</p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex justify-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)]">Get a pre-qual</a>
        <a href={`tel:${COMPANY.hq.phoneE164}`} class="btn btn-secondary !border-white !text-white">{COMPANY.hq.phone}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
